"Solutions for Advent of Code 2025, Day 05.

Part 1: Count available ingredient IDs that fall within any fresh range.

Part 2: Count total distinct IDs covered by any range. Sort ranges by
start, merge overlapping intervals, sum their lengths.

Approach: sort the ranges by start and fold them into disjoint
intervals. Part 1 keeps the intervals in a dict keyed by start and finds
the one range that could hold an ID with `dict.range`; part 2 sums the
interval lengths.

Notable eucalypt techniques:
- `split-when` to partition input at a blank line
- `bimap` to apply different parsers to each section
- `uncurry` to feed a parsed pair into a two-argument solver
- `foldl-strict` with nested list-destructuring in the step function
- `dict.range` for a predecessor lookup
- juxtaposed list-destructuring syntax `range-size[lo, hi]:`
"

parse-range(s): s str.split-on("-") map(num)

parse(data): data split-when(= "") bimap(map(parse-range), filter(!= "") ; map(num))

` "Merge a range into `[merged-so-far, current]`.  If it overlaps or is
adjacent, extend the current range; otherwise close the current range
(consing it onto `merged-so-far`) and start a fresh one."
merge-step([merged, [cur-lo, cur-hi]], [r-lo, r-hi]):
  (r-lo <= cur-hi + 1) then(
    [merged, [cur-lo, max(cur-hi, r-hi)]],
    [cons([cur-lo, cur-hi], merged), [r-lo, r-hi]])

` "Sort ranges by start, then fold to merge them into disjoint ranges,
in ascending order."
merge-ranges(ranges): {
  sorted: ranges sort-by-num(first)
  result: sorted tail foldl-strict(merge-step, [[], sorted head])
}.(cons(result second, result first) reverse)

` "Is `id` fresh?  `starts` is a dict from the start of each disjoint
range to its end, so the only range that can hold `id` is the one with
the greatest start at or below it: an O(log n) `dict.range` lookup."
fresh?(starts, id): {
  below: starts dict.range(0, id + 1)
}.(not((below dict.empty?) ∨ ((below dict.max second) < id)))

` "Count of fresh IDs, by one dict lookup per ID."
solve(ranges, ids): {
  starts: merge-ranges(ranges) dict.of
}.(ids filter(fresh?(starts)) count)

range-size[lo, hi]: hi - lo + 1

solve2(ranges): merge-ranges(ranges) map(range-size) sum

` { target: :test
    import: "ex1=inputs/day05-ex1.txt" }
test: {
  part-1: ex1 parse uncurry(solve) //= 3
  part-2: ex1 parse first solve2 //= 14
  edges: solve([[3, 5], [10, 12]], [2, 3, 5, 6, 10, 12, 13]) //= 4
  RESULT: (part-1 ∧ part-2 ∧ edges) then(:PASS, :FAIL)
}
